{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the fourth generation, etc. for it is not yet complete.</b> One must pay attention to this reasoning. Now, Rashi and Ramban disagreed in the interpretation of \"fourth generation.\" Rashi, may his memory be for blessing, explained: fourth to those who went down [to Egypt] — Judah, Perez, Hezron, and Caleb son of Hezron was among those who entered the Land. And Ramban, may his memory be for blessing, rejected Rashi's words and explained \"fourth generation\" as referring to the Amorite, for HaShem visits the iniquity of fathers upon the third and upon the fourth generation (Exodus 34:7). And it is difficult for me even according to Ramban's words, for to what purpose does HaShem inform Abraham of this thing? And furthermore, the condition is not hinted at in HaShem's words — that if the Amorite repents He will not destroy them — yet this is the essence of informing him about the \"fourth generation.\" And further, if the \"fourth generation\" is that of the Amorite, there would have been no need to go back and mention it afterward by saying \"the iniquity of the Amorite,\" since He is already speaking of them in proximity. And it is manifest that Ramban's words are not clear." "And the correct interpretation is that HaShem intended to state two appointed times: the first is the end of the exile, the servitude, and the affliction — all of which combine to the count of four hundred [years]; and when four hundred years are reached, the decree is complete, and HaShem brought them out of Egypt on the very day that the four hundred were completed. And the second appointed time is for entering the Land — that the entry into the Land would not coincide with the completion of the four hundred, but would wait until another generation, which is the fourth generation. And you should know that Caleb's generation is reckoned as the third generation, and he is the one who came out of Egypt; and although Caleb entered the Land, the generation is not counted except by its majority, and Caleb alone remained from that generation, as it is written (Numbers 14:30): ״כִּי אִם כָּלֵב בֶּן יְפֻנֶּה״. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, said \"fourth generation,\" He was speaking about the generality of Israel. And Rashi's words are not clear in what he said on this matter; rather, the \"fourth generation\" is the generation that came out of Egypt — those under twenty years of age when they entered the Land, who are Caleb's sons — and they are fourth to the decree when you count Perez, Hezron, Caleb, Jesher and Shobab, Caleb's sons: they are the fourth generation that returned to the land of Canaan. For one should not count the generations from the tribes, as Rashi began to count — Judah, Perez, etc. — for if we begin from the day the decree of four hundred began, we would count Isaac as well; and if we begin from the first man who went down to Egypt, we count from Jacob, and Caleb would be fifth. Rather, surely one should not count the generations except from when the servitude began — when Israel was no longer in their own authority — and this was after the death of the tribes, for then the servitude began, as they said, may their memory be for blessing (Exodus Rabbah 1:5), that as long as the tribes were alive they did not taste servitude, and so too with their fathers." "And HaShem hinted at the time when the counting of generations would begin by saying <b>they shall return here</b>. Note carefully the word <b>יָשׁוּבוּ</b>, to say that under compulsion by the hand of a ruler He says ״יָשׁוּבוּ״ — when they complete four generations. And the first generation over which the hand of a ruler prevailed is the sons of the tribes, for the tribes were in their own authority, and their sons returned to Egypt, not to go up from the land. And understand this, for it is the essential point." "<b>And now</b> let us look to understand the words of the Holy One, who drawers forth wisdom, in His saying <b>for the iniquity of the Amorite is not yet complete</b>. If so, it is demonstrated that the iniquity of the Amorite held back the entry. And in the narrative that HaShem arranged in His pleasant Torah, He declares that the iniquity of the spies was what caused the delay of the generation that came out of Egypt — to its very end [i.e., all of them perishing]; and their sons He brought there. And but for the spies who acted wickedly, the generation that came out would have entered in the second year after the Exodus, which is the end of the four hundred. And the spies went only to extract the desire of those who asked to spy; and but for this, HaShem had said to them that they would enter in that very generation that came out of Egypt, which is the third. And after all, the iniquity of the Amorite was not yet complete, according to His saying, blessed be He (Genesis 15:16)." "<b>However</b>, the intent of the verse is according to what they said, may their memory be for blessing, in tractate Berakhot (7a), chapter one, on the verse (Habakkuk 1:13): ״בְּבַלַּע רָשָׁע צַדִּיק מִמֶּנּוּ״ — \"a righteous man from among him\" — he swallows up; \"a completely righteous man\" — he does not swallow up — end quote. The explanation is: if the wicked man still has some merit in his hand, and a righteous man who is mostly meritorious comes to contend with him — the righteous man has no power to destroy him, for the wicked man still has the remainder of his merit to be fulfilled in this world, and the wicked man will take vengeance against the righteous man, since there is in the righteous man's hand some imperfection, for he is not a completely righteous man. But when he is complete — even though some portion of good remains to the wicked man — his totality is torn away. And likewise if the wicked man is absolute and his sentence to die is final — in everything, HaShem performs His mission to destroy him, and whoever precedes him has gained the privilege, and even one who is not a completely righteous man can destroy him from the world." "<b>And behold,</b> when Israel came forth from Egypt and when they sent the spies, they were on the level of complete tzaddikim. And although they made the Golden Calf, they were already punished for it, and the trace of it is not of sufficient measure to prevent them from bearing the name 'complete tzaddik' for the purpose of this matter — that they not be called complete tzaddikim in comparison with the Amorite. For they are the generation of the pious, as it says (Sanhedrin 110b), as it is written (Psalms 50:5): ״אִסְפוּ לִי חֲסִידָי כּוֹרְתֵי״ and so forth. But this was not so after the mission of the spies, for they provoked HaShem and rebelled, and so forth. And then they entered the category of 'tzaddik lesser than him' in comparison with the Amorite. Therefore, when they were complete tzaddikim before the spies, they had the power to dislodge the Amorite, for they would say to him: Vacate the place from our land that HaShem has given us, and if he refuses, behold he is a rebel and liable to death — which is not so after they sinned and rebelled against the words of El, for they no longer retained the presumption of a complete tzaddik. And since the iniquity of the Amorite was not yet complete, it was not in their power to destroy him. And therefore you will find that when they presumed to go up to the mountain, the Amorite came out and pursued them and struck them as bees do, and this demonstrates the swallowing up of a wicked one who is 'a tzaddik lesser than him.'" "And you will find further that at their entry Moses said (Deuteronomy 9:5): ״לֹא בְצִדְקָתְךָ וְגוֹ׳ כִּי אִם בְּרִשְׁעַת הַגּוֹיִם הָאֵלֶּה״ — because their iniquity was complete. And according to this, even though you are not complete tzaddikim, ״אַתָּה בָּא לָרֶשֶׁת״ and so forth, for he has no merit whatsoever to say of him 'a tzaddik lesser than him.' For the meaning of 'a tzaddik lesser than him' implies that he has some merit such that it is justified to call him 'a tzaddik lesser than him,' but one who is absolute in wickedness has no share of righteousness to call him 'a tzaddik lesser than him.' And understand this." "And from this the words of HaShem will be explained correctly: <b>וְדוֹר רְבִיעִי</b> and so forth — meaning: He promises a promise that cannot be annulled in any manner in the world, and He gave a reason for the matter, may His name be blessed, and in giving the reason: <b>כִּי לֹא שָׁלֵם</b> and so forth. And you will understand to know that this reason suffices only for a time when Israel was lacking the name 'complete tzaddik,' and then, on the side that the iniquity of the Amorite was not yet complete, they could not enter his land. But if Israel were on the level of a complete tzaddik, the impediment of the incompletion of the iniquity would not prevent them from entering to possess his land. And therefore when they came forth from Egypt and received the Torah, the El was willing that they enter the land immediately and take his land, for they would wage war against them and be zealous against doers of wickedness. Which is not so after the sin of the spies, for they entered the category of 'a tzaddik lesser than him'; therefore HaShem delayed until the entire generation had passed, and in the meantime He would complete the iniquity of the Amorite. And were it not for the reason of the completion of the iniquity of the Amorite, His sons would have entered immediately, and their sin would be exacted through other punishments as the Judge sees fit in justice."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {3 "Reading להפקת רצון השואלים לרגל as 'to extract/bring forth the desire of those who asked to spy,' following the root פ-ו-ק; the passage remains texturally dense and is rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:05.812124085Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}